  "final_message": "Customers now have a `net_terms_days` setting (default 7), and finalized invoices set `due_at` from the customer's current term. The full suite passes: 31 tests, the 24 existing plus 7 new ones.\n\nWhat changed:\n\n- **Model and serialization:** `Customer` gets a `net_terms_days` field, and `customer_json` includes it in responses.\n- **`customers.create_customer(..., net_terms_days=7)`** and **`customers.update_customer(..., net_terms_days=...)`** both validate the value. Negative values and non-integers raise `ValidationError`.\n- **`POST /customers`** reads `net_terms_days` from the body. A missing or null value uses 7. A negative value returns HTTP 400.\n- **`invoices.finalize`** reads the customer's term at finalization time, so a change made after the draft was created still applies.\n- **Constant rename:** `INVOICE_NET_DAYS` is now `DEFAULT_NET_TERMS_DAYS`. Nothing else referenced the old name.\n- **Tests:** new file `tests/test_net_terms.py` covers the default, custom terms on create, negative rejection on create and update, `due_at` after an update, and the API paths (201, default, and 400).\n- **`answer.json`** is written in the repo root with the one-sentence summary.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n\n- Existing customers in the store have no stored term. Because the store is in-memory with no persisted data to migrate, I did not add a migration.\n- `net_terms_days` is exposed in `customer_json`, so it also appears in `customer.created` and `customer.updated` webhook payloads.\n- The API treats an explicit `null` as \"use the default\" rather than as an error.\n\nNothing is committed.",
